.TH ar "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ar \- maintain archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBar \fIoption\fB[\fImodifier\fB] [\fIposition\fB] \fIarchive \fB[\fImember ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ar -- maintain archives
Usage:	ar option[modifier] [ position ] archive [ member ... ]
Options:
	d	Delete given members
	m	Move member to indicated position (default: end)
	p	Print members
	q	Quick append--put members at end with no checking
	r	Replace each member specified in the archive
	t	Print a table of members (default: all)
	x	Extract the specified members (default: all)
Modifiers:
	a	Place new member after 'position' in archive
	b	Place new member before 'position' in archive
	c	Suppress message when new archive is created
	i	Insert new member before 'position' in archive
	l	Use current directory for temporaries (default: '/tmp')
	u	Update--replace members only if newer than those in archive
	v	Print extra information when used with certain options
.HE
.B ar
maintains an
.I archive
(also called a
.IR library ):
a single file holding a collection of other files, called
.IR members ,
each preceded by a header giving its name, date, owner, group, mode,
and size.
The chief use of archives is to hold libraries of object files for
the linker
.BR ld ,
which understands the archive format and can search a library to
resolve undefined references.
.PP
The mandatory
.I option
argument is exactly one of the following letters, optionally
followed by one or more
.I modifier
letters, with no introducing hyphen:
.IP \fBd\fR
Delete each given
.I member
from
.IR archive .
.IP \fBm\fR
Move each given
.I member
within
.IR archive .
With no
.I modifier
governing placement, move each member to the end.
.IP \fBp\fR
Print each given
.I member
on the standard output.
.IP \fBq\fR
Quick append: put each named
.I member
at the end of
.I archive
without checking whether it is already present.
.IP \fBr\fR
Replace each named
.I member
of
.IR archive ,
or append it if not already present.
If
.I archive
does not exist,
.B ar
creates it.
.IP \fBt\fR
Print a table of the members named; if none is named, print a table
of all members.
.IP \fBx\fR
Extract each named member into the current directory; if none is
named, extract all members.
.I archive
is not changed.
.PP
Any of the following modifiers may follow the option letter:
.IP \fBa\fR
Place the new member after
.I position
in the archive (used with
.B m
or
.BR r ).
.IP \fBb\fR
Place the new member before
.I position
in the archive (used with
.B m
or
.BR r ).
.IP \fBc\fR
Suppress the message that
.B ar
normally prints when it creates a new archive.
.IP \fBi\fR
Same as the
.B b
modifier.
.IP \fBl\fR
Build the temporary file
.B ar
uses while it works in the current directory, rather than in
.BR /tmp .
.IP \fBu\fR
Replace a member only if the file named on the command line is
newer than the version already in
.IR archive .
.IP \fBv\fR
Print extra information when used with
.BR p ,
.BR t ,
.BR x ,
.BR m ,
.BR r ,
.BR d ,
or
.BR q .
.PP
Each archive begins with a magic number, followed by the members,
each preceded by an
.B ar_hdr
header giving its name, date, group and user id, mode, and size in
bytes.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ld ,
.B ranlib
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B ar
prints a diagnostic and a nonzero exit status if
.I archive
cannot be opened or created, if a named file cannot be opened, or if
an I/O error occurs; it prints the names of any requested members
that were not found.
.SH NOTES
.B ar
does not itself build or maintain a symbol-index member of an
archive; after creating or updating an archive that
.B ld
will search for symbols, run
.B ranlib
on it separately.
